{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כרע שכב כארי וכלביא מי יקימנו", :translit "kara shakhav k'ari v'lavi mi y'kimenhu", :gloss "he crouched lay down like a lion and like a lioness who will rouse him"} {:he "הן עם כלביא יקום וכארי יתנשא", :translit "hen am k'lavi yakum v'ch'ari yitnasei", :gloss "behold a people like a lioness rises and like a lion lifts itself up"} {:he "כן הדרך", :translit "chen ha-derech", :gloss "so is the way"} {:he "כלביא בימי יהושע", :translit "k'lavi bimei y'hoshua", :gloss "like a lioness in the days of Joshua"} {:he "פני משה כפני חמה", :translit "pnei moshe k'pnei chamah", :gloss "the face of Moses is like the face of the sun"} {:he "פני יהושע כפני לבנה", :translit "pnei y'hoshua k'pnei levanah", :gloss "the face of Joshua is like the face of the moon"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Order reversal of lion/lioness from Numbers 23:24 alludes to Moses (sun) vs. Joshua (moon)"}], :frame {:text "Numbers 23:24 places lioness before lion, but Numbers 24:9 reverses to lion before lioness. The sun-moon metaphor for Moses and Joshua derives from Deuteronomy 34:10, where no prophet arose like Moses, understood to mean Moses' prophecy was of a different order (Sifrei Zuta 355:8; also Yalkut Shimoni on Devarim 939). The distinction that Moses' face is like the sun and Joshua's like the moon reflects the idea that Joshua received prophecy reflected from Moses, as the moon reflects the sun.", :sources ["Numbers 23:24" "Numbers 24:9" "Deuteronomy 34:10" "Sifrei Zuta 355:8" "Yalkut Shimoni Devarim 939"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מברכיך ברוך ואורריך ארור", :translit "m'varkhecha baruch u'orercha arur", :gloss "those who bless you blessed and those who curse you cursed"} {:he "ויתן לך", :translit "v'yiten lach", :gloss "may He give you"} {:he "ארור אורריך ומברכיך ברוך", :translit "arur orercha u'm'varkhecha baruch", :gloss "cursed be those who curse you and those who bless you blessed"} {:he "ברח לך", :translit "barach lach", :gloss "flee for yourself"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Reversal of blessing-curse order between Balaam (Numbers 24:9) and Isaac (Genesis 27:29) reveals Balaam's hostile intent"}], :frame {:text "Balaam's statement in Numbers 24:9 places the curse last, unlike Isaac's blessing in Genesis 27:29 which places the curse first. Numbers 24:10-11 narrate that Balak's anger was kindled at Balaam, and Balak told him to flee. The Sages state that from the heart's intention one can discern inner malice even in outwardly positive speech (Berakhot 28a).", :sources ["Numbers 24:9" "Genesis 27:29" "Numbers 24:10-11" "Berakhot 28a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Order reversal of lion/lioness from Numbers 23:24 alludes to Moses (sun) vs. Joshua (moon)", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Reversal of blessing-curse order between Balaam (Numbers 24:9) and Isaac (Genesis 27:29) reveals Balaam's hostile intent", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:31:23.203427020Z", :model "glm-5.2"}